    "description": "Iran is a major steel producer with a substantial direct-reduced iron industry. Understanding its steel sector requires distinguishing ironmaking, crude steel production, and the rolling and finishing of steel products.",
    "tags": ["Steel", "Direct-reduced iron", "Electric arc furnaces", "Manufacturing", "Industrial materials"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T11:39:54Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T11:39:54Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Steel in Iran: Production, Processes and Industrial Connections",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s steel industry, its direct-reduced iron production, electric furnace processes, and the difference between crude steel and finished products."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An industry with several distinct stages",
        "content": "Iran’s steel industry is internationally significant: the World Steel Association places the country among the leading crude steel producers. Its industrial importance extends upstream into ironmaking and downstream into products used in construction and manufacturing. These stages belong to a connected production system, but their output figures describe different materials and should not be added together. [s1][s3]\n\nSteel is an iron-based alloy whose composition and processing determine its properties. It appears in structures, vehicles, machinery and household equipment. A national steel-production total therefore describes the scale of an industrial material, rather than a single standardized finished product. It does not reveal the mix of grades or the suitability of particular mills for demanding applications. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "production-routes",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why direct-reduced iron matters",
        "content": "Direct-reduced iron, or DRI, is an ironmaking product used as a metallic input for steelmaking. Iran’s substantial DRI production makes this intermediate material particularly useful for understanding the country’s industrial chain. Worldsteel’s production-by-process table also identifies electric furnaces as the dominant steelmaking route in Iran. [s1]\n\nAn electric arc furnace melts metallic feedstock using electricity and supplementary fuel. Its charge can include scrap, DRI and pig iron; the furnace itself is not equivalent to an ironmaking plant. The alternative integrated route produces molten iron in a blast furnace and converts it into steel in a basic oxygen furnace. These distinctions explain why electricity supply, metallic feedstock and upstream iron production all matter when assessing a steelworks. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "dri-trend",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iran’s direct-reduced iron production, 2021–2025",
          "dataset_id": "iran-dri",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["dri-output"],
          "x_axis_label": "Gregorian calendar year",
          "y_axis_label": "Million metric tonnes",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "Reported DRI production increased in every year of this five-year series. DRI is an intermediate iron product; this trend is not a measure of finished steel output or domestic steel consumption. [s1]"
        },
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "products",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From crude steel to useful products",
        "content": "After steelmaking, casting produces intermediate shapes such as slabs, billets and blooms. Rolling then transforms them into flat products, including plate and coils, or long products such as reinforcing bar, wire rod and structural sections. Further operations can include cold rolling, coating and painting. [s3]\n\nThe distinction is practical when reading Iranian industry announcements. A new rolling line changes downstream processing capability; it does not necessarily create additional crude steel. Similarly, more DRI capacity does not establish how much steel a furnace actually produced. Installed capacity, actual output and finished-product shipments answer different questions. The production stages provide the framework for keeping those measures separate. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "energy-and-assessment",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Assessing performance beyond tonnage",
        "content": "Electric furnace production should not automatically be described as low-carbon steel. Its environmental performance depends partly on the electricity used and on how metallic inputs were produced. DRI and pig iron carry upstream emissions, while alloy additions can introduce further impacts. A plant-level assessment therefore needs evidence about the full production route. [s2]\n\nFor readers comparing Iran with other producers, the most useful starting point is to identify the stage being measured. DRI output describes ironmaking, crude steel describes steelmaking, and rolled products describe subsequent transformation. Keeping those boundaries visible makes both industrial expansion claims and environmental comparisons more meaningful. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
